  "subtitle": "A floor-roving infrared-controlled robot that made Logo programs physically visible",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Valiant Turtle makes code into a physical event. A child types FORWARD 50 RIGHT 90 on a BBC Micro, and a 25cm hemisphere on the classroom floor actually moves forward 50cm and turns right 90 degrees. The interaction is Papert's 'body-syntonic' reasoning made literal: the program IS the turtle's motion. No other artifact in the museum captures this code-to-physical-movement paradigm — Topo is a programmable output device without sensors, HERO 1 is a sensor-equipped teaching robot with onboard keyboard, but the Valiant Turtle is the purest expression of body-syntonic computing: the child's own body schema becomes the debugger.",
